Thanks @nicolas-grekas!

**Meta vs Alt**: confirmed on the branch. With Terminal.app's "Use Option as Meta key", `StdinBuffer` turns the high byte into `ESC x`, which parses as `alt+x`, so `matches("\ex", 'meta+x')` is false. The kitty alt form (`\e[120;3u`) doesn't match `meta+x` either; only the kitty Meta bit (`\e[120;33u`) does, on terminals that report it. I'll send a symfony-docs PR for `tui/topics/keybindings.rst` listing the new modifiers, with a warning that what readline and terminal settings call "Meta" is `alt` here.

**Rejecting unknown modifiers**: agreed, done in [b1f8f651104](https://github.com/symfony/symfony/pull/66538/commits/b1f8f6511048441702c903c3e5b123c9a00ebfda). On 8.2 an unknown modifier was dropped, so `ctlr+x` or `cmd+x` fired on a plain `x`; this PR already made it match nothing, and `Keybindings` now throws an `InvalidArgumentException` when it's created with such a key id (or one with no key, like `ctrl+`). `KeyParser::matches()` itself still returns false, so raw `InputEvent` checks are unchanged.

`KeybindingsTrait::getKeybindings()` rebuilds a `Keybindings` on each input, so I measured it: with `EditorWidget`'s 41 default key ids, construction takes about 15 µs, so I didn't add caching.

Typos in key names (`escpe`, `ctrl+escpe`) still match nothing silently. Catching those needs the parser to expose the key names it knows, so I'd rather do it in a follow-up.

